  "answer": {
    "response": "`Invoice.totalCents` has one persistence definition, one write path, one explicit read/API path, one queue input mapping, and one direct test.\n\n- Persistence: `Invoice.totalCents` is a TypeORM `integer` column on `invoices`. A database type/name change requires the corresponding schema migration, although no migration files exist in this fixture.\n- Write path: `InvoiceProcessor.process` reads `GenerateInvoiceJob.totalCents` and passes it to `InvoiceService.createInvoice`. The service creates `{ customer, totalCents }` and saves it through `Repository<Invoice>`.\n- Read path: `InvoiceService.findById` loads the invoice with `findOne`; because no projection excludes the column, `totalCents` is hydrated with the entity. `InvoiceResolver.invoice` then explicitly reads it.\n- API mapping: `InvoiceResolver.invoice` manually maps the entity to `{ id, totalCents }`. A rename or representation change must be reflected here. The existing query decorator declares `String` while returning this object, an existing GraphQL contract mismatch worth resolving when changing the field.\n- Queue contract: `GenerateInvoiceJob.totalCents` is an upstream serialized contract. Renaming or changing its type requires coordinated producers and existing queued-job compatibility, though no invoice producer is present in the fixture.\n- Tests: `invoice.service.test.ts` passes `4200` and asserts the saved result contains `totalCents: 4200`; update this test for any property/type/normalization change. There are no direct processor, resolver/API, database-mapping, or migration tests covering the other affected surfaces.\n\n`Order.totalCents` and its tests are a separate entity/path with no source-level coupling to `Invoice`, so they are not affected unless the intended change is a shared business-wide money-contract change.\n\nIf only the physical database column name changes while the entity property remains `totalCents` via an explicit TypeORM column-name mapping, the service, queue, resolver, and test contracts can remain unchanged; a property or value-type change affects all surfaces above.",
